#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "8914449a-3d6f-5847-9e94-3be0a2ccbc94" :question/math-academy-id "e-9178" :question/problem "![](images/85/859a831dcc11fa6ffe430cc913fa46283d3231816429d3fea98219fe3417b746.png)\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n\n- $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n![](images/a1/a172ba62aed6c25fc1b40fdf70b1ae88a7e33d2f3341696783b96defd927b6be.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d8798f55-f005-5019-bf47-349e43237888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9179" :question/problem "The table below describes how the customers of a particular electronics store feel about their next computer purchase.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Laptop** | **Desktop** |\n| To: | **Laptop** | $0.9$ | $0.8$ |\n| **Desktop** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nIt is found that $10 \\%$ of customers who currently use a laptop would choose a desktop on their next purchase, while $80 \\%$ of customers who currently use a desktop would choose a laptop on their next purchase. \n\nGiven that the store has $5\\,400$ customers, all of whom purchase a new computer every two years, how many customers would eventually own a desktop computer if the given probabilities remain constant over many two-year periods?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n![](images/19/1935c4960a48e2b446c2610b9edc2a160a5f45462494c72a90f139ea2d13cc34.png)\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many two-year periods, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](images/29/29b5a209bb27b24f165ee418326763d8fb24f83f2b3a767052c381aa8cd0e01c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=8{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](images/0e/0e8f620c3392dbb77b1353184e38eaf48b526aa82c502772a5582bd20c7f5aea.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur steady-state vector is\n![](images/2e/2ed7b0fc7296b2b651909c7c15c73e253fc8a9f95fb037072228d5de6f838894.png)\n\nSo, ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{1}{9}}}$ of the $5\\,400$ customers would eventually own a desktop computer. Therefore, the total number of people who would eventually own a desktop computer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\cdot 5\\,400 = 600.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2164d0ba-43d5-53c7-adf1-9e6810b316a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9180" :question/problem "![](images/09/093de94431946cb83e7cc0523405989b7a23cc8d9677bdecdfba7aaf207fd62b.png)\n\nFind the steady-state vector $\\mathbf q$ of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n![](images/5e/5ed2f76d9b2857134a5b2aa5781711f9625cabb46a9ca3c3d54aa31c32bb4dbe.png)\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.3 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=20{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=20{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 8 & -12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+9{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-7{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 57 & -19 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -57 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2x_1 -7\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\\right) + x_3 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1 = \\dfrac{2}{3}x_3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n![](images/9a/9ae5533dc825d2d3d08ca7a54c50578e21dda8fad7988b9043baaad2a69368b7.png)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n![](images/fd/fd7775b5d982d0bc51e80417a70b67dbff0176afcc5ff9aec613947076201386.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e87c8124-0ff2-547b-8e5b-1dd24c33ff63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459395} {:answer/id #uuid "1db6c273-8e01-545f-91b6-8ebdebf4da20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1db6c273-8e01-545f-91b6-8ebdebf4da20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459396} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff666d64-9efb-5fa3-a856-3094022b00fa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df9a2b8-8bba-539f-90aa-2cc738bd7714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459397} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac0ac9d-8b8c-5d0a-9d3f-28652b7eb407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac0ac9d-8b8c-5d0a-9d3f-28652b7eb407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459398} :answer-field/id #uuid "c610aaa1-fb31-5f5b-906e-c90bf52d5364" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449c8856-7e56-53ff-9d15-616c80d5a4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459399} {:answer/id #uuid "029706cb-7965-5a82-8339-455757b093e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029706cb-7965-5a82-8339-455757b093e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459400} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba85054f-f2a2-5c15-b38d-4e5781404347" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3662430d-84f0-5632-b066-9a7b273fafeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110189" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1.4}{15}+\\frac{5.6}{15} \\\\ \\frac{5.6}{15}+\\frac{2.4}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c39d6007-3d64-5685-b8c2-3bdda3ca55e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459401} {:answer/id #uuid "13c1dc0d-cdb6-5c69-8cf6-39d5bc66cef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c1dc0d-cdb6-5c69-8cf6-39d5bc66cef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459402} :answer-field/id #uuid "959b7ae6-a403-5443-8e59-429047b0a0f0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a223a2e-adf2-5b55-8492-76af71dae7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459403} {:answer/id #uuid "5c89a81b-e4dc-5955-8a3f-a64c32b52cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a223a2e-adf2-5b55-8492-76af71dae7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459403} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f2897a-a72c-566d-a233-47a13942ed65" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0493696-0e98-587f-9bf7-c908798a7c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459405} {:answer/id #uuid "e539bf2b-6167-5a95-961d-e33b0f253ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0493696-0e98-587f-9bf7-c908798a7c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459405} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd17c31-ac61-58e8-b271-80f4eeae1164" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d2ea1fa-42c3-5215-bede-8b745015a967" :question/math-academy-id "q-110239" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+\\frac{1.5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1.5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245874df-8c61-5865-89e0-2eea58d0ddb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244636} {:answer/id #uuid "89ef273f-1ef4-5830-8401-02da27fbfef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245524} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0491b5-2aa5-5777-aa10-2469c7c918ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245561} {:answer/id #uuid "b33e12af-527b-59a1-a8b0-87c0b7d8c14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245909} {:answer/id #uuid "d997fab9-f7e0-5e91-9d3c-7082d52b45a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ef273f-1ef4-5830-8401-02da27fbfef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245524} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0a3814-685f-5c2a-a989-456250f0e6e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "496d0736-5684-529d-8dd0-8b061c9a6089" :question/math-academy-id "q-110242" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f53f7e-0acf-53ad-bb28-7cfb9124883a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121242} {:answer/id #uuid "b48fbcca-07de-5b03-88a9-e02fa0e16d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121243} {:answer/id #uuid "cf01b819-643f-5aa3-b9a8-ebd9af5e2416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121244} {:answer/id #uuid "18f84519-38c2-5381-b81a-655b79d8dca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121245} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7044dc-c180-54a0-8f95-b3c46d0b640d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121246}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0f6e5f-6571-5dc2-aafe-8dc89196cf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121241} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a2da09-ade9-5f02-bc30-6ec862b4c502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399728} {:answer/id #uuid "1f57ef16-daf6-55cf-b3cb-f0b639c205a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399729} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cdee49-7ce2-5cce-acee-cd378fd6b19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399730} {:answer/id #uuid "4c833b2d-9013-504c-b338-e5d0dc7d7a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399731} {:answer/id #uuid "11a1059c-7f59-5f04-b1af-4e76b82a2739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33a2da09-ade9-5f02-bc30-6ec862b4c502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399728} :answer-field/id #uuid "6866c1e5-6616-5f78-9eb2-94b16182edd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57c92fa7-df07-5594-a6b7-bd705bfe80a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-111172" :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50cf12ba-0f8c-5d6a-86d7-d5466a1a1711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464030} {:answer/id #uuid "766369ab-0fe7-5050-9921-76a53d28350f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "766369ab-0fe7-5050-9921-76a53d28350f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464031} :answer-field/id #uuid "5420eb4b-5e25-5cf6-9eef-93eee3f6b1a1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464949} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eecec88c-b638-5721-a82f-fe220b0509b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464032} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6494ed-0c0c-56f7-8e6b-f618075d7c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eecec88c-b638-5721-a82f-fe220b0509b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464032} :answer-field/id #uuid "173bb258-7b2f-58fe-9890-bd42b84021c2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2200c26f-db61-5766-ace1-134df5c653ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464034} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3ffa9e-833e-593a-9dd3-7bae916e93ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2200c26f-db61-5766-ace1-134df5c653ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464034} :answer-field/id #uuid "937a727a-ea73-5f1f-8418-c0da8ee8dea3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56012d7d-0dc5-5803-80a1-167efecb090a" :question/math-academy-id "q-135307" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16370931-5fc9-5536-9fd7-45afe61a10b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472688} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab6d543-ae19-5030-b18a-58051110b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ab6d543-ae19-5030-b18a-58051110b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472689} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4b12e7-31db-52dd-a206-759d60508923" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71dd4b86-fcee-58b2-a783-19a83e01d1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472690} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7a9115-7513-51f1-a218-6359bc259d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71dd4b86-fcee-58b2-a783-19a83e01d1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472690} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc16e75-61f9-56a2-8f15-331022147565" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3605e075-25c5-54da-bf16-7e2307304916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472692} {:answer/id #uuid "9db4a1e3-8985-5443-8d1e-4853b29ea3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3605e075-25c5-54da-bf16-7e2307304916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472692} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb71628d-05cf-5ce1-8a2b-c44a3882f058" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62944003-1237-5928-b405-2159dbe448f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-135308" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.03+0.72 \\\\ 0.07+0.18\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e0631e-07fa-5259-8791-2c5926d4ae5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266359} {:answer/id #uuid "5686887c-bf1f-5eef-8d6c-4c47be372a18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266913} {:answer/id #uuid "89d0ef52-9998-5e57-ac2e-1c6860957c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267370} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0a0335-537e-50c2-9b17-5c27afb509f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267419} {:answer/id #uuid "9726d41f-9c36-579a-af0e-92267c382c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9726d41f-9c36-579a-af0e-92267c382c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267496} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f90970-79cf-5206-a259-f9ec67ec053f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e24f728-ce2c-55b9-a28e-e90dcc5c4465" :question/math-academy-id "q-135325" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 \\\\ 1 & -0.3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{13}{10}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{10}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{13} \\\\ \\frac{10}{13}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{13}-\\frac{10}{13}=-\\frac{7}{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8759a573-7553-5834-adc7-bb7ecebc071a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267341} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0e5979-4ac9-5e3d-9de3-365e6465dfa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267420} {:answer/id #uuid "96650908-cc82-5cc1-a604-e073fc34db3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267486} {:answer/id #uuid "a4065787-991b-5abe-8f52-e0833ba0558a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267621} {:answer/id #uuid "fed65815-0129-53dc-b1d7-9c523cd86a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fed65815-0129-53dc-b1d7-9c523cd86a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268388} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee78886-ce53-5ed1-a620-8c46b1165547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69190782-47c3-5351-a63c-037e5f837593" :question/math-academy-id "q-135332" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 \\\\ 1 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{5}{8}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b373c41-8b42-50f5-90d0-b51a7c83360c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266524} {:answer/id #uuid "d011fc48-45b4-58ae-99ed-0034c009ba75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268004} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb9f8b1-5a19-57d1-ab57-3438a2df3158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268101} {:answer/id #uuid "f28e16fb-1222-5743-b296-45c4f2b1221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268300} {:answer/id #uuid "ff283d97-948c-5367-8a78-e4d3b3cf4c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f28e16fb-1222-5743-b296-45c4f2b1221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268300} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcddcb02-38f8-5b6f-bc4a-2daf7cc42e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26bfb2a9-4e8e-55d5-877c-2b96948f29b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-135344" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.2 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.25\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.2 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & -0.8 & 0.65 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & -0.75 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 6.5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -7.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}+0.5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fa980f-8d16-5c0b-adc6-32aaa0351649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411597} {:answer/id #uuid "3f726a36-e309-5b98-8554-2e43c91e6dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411601} {:answer/id #uuid "47f65641-4908-5c8e-b21e-1a448edd51ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411602} {:answer/id #uuid "49e05de2-77df-5db3-a385-91d68a9be627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411603} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d1b46f-e211-5172-be37-ae8796943205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f726a36-e309-5b98-8554-2e43c91e6dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411601} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e744f0f-1893-571c-a245-886e0be7796d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15361c81-aace-5a26-b7c5-2e82d38f451f" :question/math-academy-id "q-135349" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 19 & -19 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb2fe76-32b7-514f-bb2e-89698a403db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266845} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd5a800-a03a-5e01-ba96-0ac9151231ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266990} {:answer/id #uuid "a117550e-deb7-5fd6-bd07-a00068e6e499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267596} {:answer/id #uuid "b94b7abd-5692-581d-9a3c-f648ded28ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267806} {:answer/id #uuid "c487e502-1285-5b73-b57b-667e8d4dcbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94b7abd-5692-581d-9a3c-f648ded28ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267806} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4613a71-c841-55fc-9277-7af5997d67da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "523b5c51-5d69-5337-be0d-a6c9d40f6678" :question/math-academy-id "q-135352" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.4 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -14 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{14}{10}{x}_{3}=\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2(\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{20}{5}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{20} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{7}{20}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{10}{20}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7a7e5d-76cc-5a5b-8fc9-fc2d065c203d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267237} {:answer/id #uuid "7f20b487-b010-568a-a779-aeb7ba3871af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267263} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c78b2a-fdee-5ed0-8adf-6c7047ded625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267778} {:answer/id #uuid "e2323f2b-578f-5ade-a62f-5a863e325945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268142} {:answer/id #uuid "e833fd16-0dbc-5db9-8c59-dd1eeea98194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7a7e5d-76cc-5a5b-8fc9-fc2d065c203d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267237} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f9d971-672b-52c6-a33e-21c8b357ea30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2ead374-20fc-5336-8f26-ef768edbfcce" :question/math-academy-id "q-135408" :question/problem "There are two groups of people In a small town: those who travel to work by car and those who travel by train. Every year a study is done in which people are asked about their preference between using trains versus cars. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Car** | **Train** |\n| To: | **Car** | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Train** | $0.65$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $65%$ of people who currently travel by car will switch to train next year, while $25%$ of people who currently travel by train will switch to their car next year.\n\nAssuming that the town has $90\\,000$ people that travel to work by train or car, and the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many people will eventually use the train to travel to work?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.35 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0.65 & -0.25 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.65 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{18}{13}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{13}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{13}{18}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{13}{18}$ of the people will eventually use the train to travel. Therefore, the number of people who will eventually use the train is\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{18}\\cdot 90\\,000=65\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea59a55-ca8c-5011-94bb-adc5e2783f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388822} {:answer/id #uuid "8e324f12-a685-504b-be59-f46dd3f2728f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "340" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388823} {:answer/id #uuid "02091894-fa17-5170-9a72-9c82ed0e6bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388824} {:answer/id #uuid "d0746dbd-50e5-51db-b5aa-9a4c741c97d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388825} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c66c2-72df-5f5b-aab3-269b3148afdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69c66c2-72df-5f5b-aab3-269b3148afdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388826} :answer-field/id #uuid "c17d838d-64b9-5408-9e52-5f894639d0cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a031dbeb-978c-5542-b4e9-e4e60f2dc984" :question/math-academy-id "q-141024" :question/problem "A study conducted on $3\\,400$ teenagers by the transportation department of a particular city reveals that teenagers' preference for the type of home-to-school transportation changes with time. The following table shows the results of this study.  \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Bus** | **Bicycle** | **Electric Scooter ** |\n| To: | **Bus** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Bicycle** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Electric Scooter** | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of teenagers who used to go to school by bus now go by bicycle.  \n\nHow many teenagers would eventually go to school on electric scooters if the listed probabilities remained constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157f5ca9-b81c-53c8-b1d3-29be34acd7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269453} {:answer/id #uuid "21680cff-4228-5c00-9484-24e5b719268c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269552} {:answer/id #uuid "3728440d-e1a2-5207-95ac-9416bd057cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269768} {:answer/id #uuid "a163dbe7-d913-56ca-a615-fbf7e9977ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270700} {:answer/id #uuid "d28a917b-3f74-5562-b106-c78d82823886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d28a917b-3f74-5562-b106-c78d82823886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271123} :answer-field/id #uuid "264e87e8-2ef0-5cf0-82c6-75d0173c6ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03023a20-ec6d-5cf1-9ba8-6fa6f21acb01" :question/math-academy-id "q-141035" :question/problem "A study shows how the sports preferences of young people in a certain community vary from year to year. The following table shows the results of this study. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Baseball** | **Basketball** | **Football** |\n| To: | **Baseball** | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n| **Basketball** | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Football** | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nFor example, $10%$ of people who used to prefer baseball now prefer football. \n\nGiven that $1\\,260$ young people were interviewed for the study and that the probabilities listed remain constant for many years, how many of those people will eventually prefer baseball?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & -0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & -0.2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{2}{7}$ of the people will prefer baseball. Therefore, the number of people who will prefer baseball is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot 1\\,260=360\\,\\text{people}.\n$$"}]}
